## INTRODUCTION
Metal-oxo clusters isolated from water are akin to molecular metal oxides in their role as active catalysts,1–3 sorbents for sequestering aqueous contaminants,4–6 and materials for energy production and storage.7 In their molecular form, metal-oxo clusters are ideal for elucidating reaction pathways of materials assembled from water, where cluster topology may predicate material form and function.8–11 Metal-oxo clusters are long-known modular building blocks for zeolites,12,13 and more recently for thin films,14,15 porous frameworks,16 and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs).17,18
Cluster-topologies that self-assemble in aqueous $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ solutions are limited in scope. The $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ clusters include tetrameric and hexameric forms; the former is ubiquitous in aqueous hydrohalic acid, and uncertain in perchloric acid solution.19,20 The Clearfield-Vaughan tetramer $(\mathbf{Zr}_{4})$ , dubbed as such in reference to its initial isolation, $21-24$ is an acidic polycation formulated $\mathrm{[Zr_{4}(O H)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{16}]^{8+}}$ (Figure 1), commonly described and sold commercially as $\mathrm{\phantom{-}2r O C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O^{\prime}}$ . The hexamer topology, consisting of a $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(O,O H)_{8}}$ core with antiprismatic- $\cdot\boldsymbol{Z}\mathbf{r}$ polyhedra arranged in a pseudo-octahedron, presumably requires a bridging ligand to convert $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ to $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ in water.25 $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ has been widely exploited in MOFs,26 now commercially available as UiO66. Both $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs and $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ are robust and inexpensive acid catalysts. $27^{-29}$ Despite the importance of these clusters, there has been minimal success in expanding the family of $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ -polyoxohydroxocations. In addition to growing our fundamental knowledge of aqueous group IV chemistry, new $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ cluster topologies could lead to new MOF reticulations from pre-formed clusters, and new homogenous and heterogeneous acid catalysts. Additionally, an improved understanding and control of aqueous speciation of $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ can innovate new separations chemistries30,31 and control $Z\mathbf{r}$ in solvent extraction processes, both critical for nuclear technologies.
Combining sulfate with $\mathrm{Hf/Zr_{4}}$ in aqueous acid yields several cluster forms with nuclearities of 11, 17 and $18.^{32-35}$ However, these clusters are largely insoluble, due to the network-forming and neutralizing tendency of divalent sulfate. High-resolution lithography is achieved from acidic $\mathrm{Zr/HfOCl_{2}}$ -sulfate-peroxide deposition solutions. The peroxide is converted to oxide upon radiation exposure and provides solubility contrast to create patterned coatings for fabrication of microelectronic devices.36 From highly alkaline solution, we have isolated a Hf-hexamer ring linked by bridging peroxide,37 which provided a model for understanding the development chemistry of $\mathrm{{HfO}}_{2}$ lithography. Aside from the radiation response of the peroxide in lithography, its coordination structure and chemistry in the acidic deposition solutions and films has not been elucidated, due to lack of relevant structural information. However, recent X-ray scattering data suggest that the sulfate and/or peroxide dissociates the $\mathrm{Zr/Hf_{4}}$ cluster via labile coordination;38 but the separate roles of sulfate and peroxide in speciation  cannot be decoupled. Moreover, we do not understand why the maximum peroxide that can bind to $\mathrm{Hf/Zr}$ in these deposition solutions is $\mathbf{1}{:}1,^{38}$ where increased peroxide should
correlate with improved radiation sensitivity. To elucidate the structure and reactivity roles of peroxide in these deposition solutions, we have targeted isolation of $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ clusters from acidic-peroxide solutions. In addition to achieving this goal, these studies have provided a means to open up the perpetually limited family of $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ aqueous polynuclear clusters. Here we describe a synthetic pathway to obtain group IV peroxide clusters that are completely inorganic and simple in composition (containing only $\mathrm{Zr/O/H})$ and highly soluble in both water and organic solvents. We present two structurally characterized clusters – $\begin{array}{r l}{[\mathrm{Zr}_{25}\mathrm{O}_{10}(\mathrm{OH})_{50}(\mathrm{O}_{2})_{5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{40}]^{20+}}&{{}(\mathrm{Zr}_{25})}\end{array}$ and $[\mathrm{Zr}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\upmu\mathrm{-O}_{2})_{2}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{12}]^{6+}\cdot(\mathrm{ZrT}_{\mathrm{d}})$ , as well as subunits of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ described by small-angle $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray scattering (SAXS) and electrospray ionization mass spectrscopy (ESIMS). Both are isolated from a parent solution of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ with peroxide, where an interplay of $\mathrm{Zr}$ concentration and the ratio of peroxide: $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ dictates the obtained structure $\mathbf{\Gamma}(\mathbf{fig1})$ , and correlates with the proposed disassembly-reassembly processes. $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ is by far the largest $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ polycation cluster reported, while $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ is an oxo-centered tetrahedron, a similar topology observed previously for Th(IV).39 Intriguingly, subunits of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ appear to assemble directly in the solid-state lattice from water, and redissolve as these same subunits, a rare crystal growth process for polynuclear clusters. These clusters, their assembly pathways, and their solution behavior provide a path forward for diversifying aqueous polycation cluster chemistry, important in cluster-based materials such as MOFs. They also serve as models for elucidating peroxidebased lithography processes.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis and structure descriptions of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ and $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$
In order to isolate peroxide-containing clusters, it is important to eliminate all redox-active species in the reaction solution, otherwise side reactions complicate the peroxide-promoted cluster disassembly and reassembly processes, or the peroxide may be decomposed altogether. Therefore, we replace the commonly-used $\mathrm{\small{~ZrOCl}}_{2}^{\prime}$ with a perchlorate salt of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ . This was achieved by dissolving basic zirconium carbonate in perchloric acid to prepare the reaction solution (see experimental section for details). Evaporation of this acidic zirconium perchlorate solution yields dendritic colorless crystals. Single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray analysis revealed the well-known $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ motif (Figure 1) in a tetragonal unit cell with completely disordered perchlorate counterions and a formulation of $\mathrm{Zr_{4}(O H)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{16}(C l O_{4})_{8}\cdot1.33H_{2}O}$ (Table $\mathbf{SI}_{2}$ ). The 1.33 $H_{2}O$ molecules per $\{\mathbf{Zr}_{4}\}$ unit have been modeled with the “solvent mask” method in Olex2 software, similar to PLATON SQUEEZE. $^{40}$ More details about the solvent and counterion disorder are given in the SI. Relevant atomic distances in $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ and in the $\mathrm{\mathop{ZrOCl}}_{2}^{0}$ commercial product $(\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ with eight chloride counterions) are identical within ${\sim}0.{\dot{\mathrm{o}}}4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , see Table SI2. The exception is the O-O distances of the bridging hydroxide ligands. With the Cl- counterion, these ligands are ${\sim}0.12\mathrm{~\AA~}$ further apart, promoted by Hbonding to the counterion (Cl---H-O). The oxygen-atoms of the perchlorate ligand do not associate with $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ leading to observed disorder in the structure. Importantly, the structure of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ along with prior-reported structures with halide, asserts the ubiquity of this $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ -polycation, regardless of the counterion, mineral acid, or electrolyte solution.
$\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ is obtained from a 10:1 solution of peroxide:Zr and a 0.5 molar $\mathrm{Zr}$ solution.  Crystals form after approximately 20 days and $80\%$ evaporation of the reaction solution (see experimental section for details). $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ is fully formulated $\mathrm{Zr_{4}(O H)_{4}(\upmu\mathrm{-O_{2})_{2}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O)(H_{2}O)_{12}\cdot(C l O_{4})_{6}\cdot4H_{2}O}}}$ (Figure 1). The $\upmu_{4}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{O}$ centered $\mathrm{Zr}$ -tetrahedron $(\mathrm{Zr-O}_{\mathrm{oxo}}\ =\ 2.110(3)\cdot$ - $2.138(_{3})\mathrm{~\AA~}$ has four bridging hydroxyls $(\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-OH}}=2.\mathrm{11}6(3).$ - $2.146(_{3})\mathrm{~\AA})$ and two bridging peroxides $(\mathrm{Zr}{\mathrm{-O}}_{\mathrm{p}}=2.136(3)\cdots$ 一 $2.173\bigl(3\AA\bigr)\$ along the six edges of the tetrahedron, and the molecule has $C_{2}$ symmetry. Three terminal aqua ligands cap each of the four $Z\mathrm{r}$ $(\mathrm{Zr{-}O_{a q u a}=2.191(3){\overline{{{-2.265(3)}}}}~\AA)}$ , completing a distorted square antiprism geometry. All six perchlorate anions are located in the lattice along with four water molecules. The bridging rather than terminallycoordinated peroxide observed in both $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ and $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ (below) provides structural evidence that peroxide dissociates $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ suggesting a simple route to activate this ubiquitous specie and broaden the scope of $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ metaloxo cluster chemistry.
Addition of peroxide to the zirconium carbonateperchloric acid solution with a 1:1 peroxide:Zr ratio, 1 molar Zr solution (see experimental section) followed by evaporation for four days $(\sim50\%$ loss of solvent volume) yields prism-shaped crystals of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}.$ , which assembles in an orthorhombic space group Cmcm (Table SI2). The cluster formulation $\mathrm{[Zr_{25}O_{10}(O H)_{50}(O_{2})_{5}(H_{2}O)_{40}]^{20+}}$ was determined by assigning hydroxyl and oxo ligands based on bond valence sum calculations (BVS), since no $\mathrm{H}$ -atoms are observed in the electron density map (Table $\mathbf{SI}_{3}$ ). The pentagonal topology of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ (Figure 1) can be described as an inner $5$ -membered ring connected to the outer ring via $\upmu_{3}\mathbf{oxo}$ and hydroxyl groups $\left(\mathrm{Zr-O}=2.083(4)–2.119(3)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and $2.369(3){-}2.3\dot{8}2(4)\ \dot{\bar{\mathrm{A}}},$ , respectively). The Zr atoms are linked within the ring via two bridging hydroxyl groups, similar to that observed in $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ . Disorder is observed for the entire inner ring (Figure SI1).
The external ring consists of five tetrameric units (Figure SI2) analogous to $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ but with slight distortions. The original $\{\mathrm{Zr_{4}(O H)_{8}}\}$ unit is a square $\mathrm{{Zr-Zr}}$ distances $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ $3{\cdot}55\mathring\mathrm{A})$ with $Z\mathbf{r}$ -OH bond distances of $2.13\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ . In $\mathbf{Zr}_{25},$ , the $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH}$ distances are close to those found in $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ (2.12-2.14 Å). However, there are two distinct $Z\mathrm{r}–Z\mathrm{r}$ distances; $3.62$ and 3.44 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ defining a rectangle. The short $Z\mathrm{r}–\mathrm{Zr}$ edge has a single oxo linkage that bridges to a $Z\mathrm{r}$ of the inner ring. Likewise, ${\upmu}_{3}$ -hydroxyl groups (bridging the inner ring) lead to the longer $Z\mathrm{r}–Z\mathrm{r}$ distance on the adjacent side of the square. These five tetrameric units are linked together by disordered peroxo and a pair of hydroxyl groups with an occupancy of 0.5 for each. A short $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ distance $(2.06-2.1\$ Å) is attributed to the presence of a peroxo group whereas a longer bond distance (2.16-2.21 Å) corresponds to OH groups. The distorted square antiprism geometry of the outer $\mathrm{Zr}$ is completed by two terminal water molecules. The $\mathrm{Zr}$ atoms of the inner ring exhibit the same coordination geometry, but bonded to only oxo and hydroxyl ligands. In its crystalline lattice, $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ clusters are oriented along the (ab) plane (Figure 2). PLATON indicates $48.8\%$ of the lattice is considered to be voids. The distance between clusters in the ab plane is ${\sim}3{\cdot}5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , measured from the terminal water ligands. The closest interlayer distance between clusters in the c-direction is ${\sim}6$ Å. Between these layers, many disordered $H_{2}O$ and $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ molecules reside without strong association to each other or the clusters. Details of modeling this disorder are summarized in the SI.
Solution characterization and disassemblyreassembly processes. Crystals of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{4},\ \mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{25},$ and $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ perchlorate salts are highly soluble in water and in organic solvents, permitting detailed solution analyses. We analyzed 50 mM $(\mathrm{Zr})$ solutions of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{4},\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{25},$ and $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ in water, MeOH, and DMF by small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS), and diluted the aqueous and MeOH solutions for electrospray ionization mass spectral analyses (ESI-MS, see SI).
The scattering curves for $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ in water, methanol, and DMF compared to simulated scattering of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ (Figure $\mathbf{3A}\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) reveals larger species than a tetramer, evidenced by the shift of the Guinier region $(\mathbf{o.l}\mathbf{-0.4\mathring{A}^{-1}})$ to lower-q. On the other hand, simulated scattering data from a model of three linked tetramers (Figure $\mathbf{3B}_{\cdot}^{\mathbf{\dot{B}}}$ ) provided a good match, suggesting ladder-type polymerization in solution. In all three solutions, there is a broad ‘diffraction peak’ at $\mathbf{q}{\sim}1.8$ $\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{\scriptscriptstyle-1}$ that matches the simulation.  This is consistent with the
3.5 Å $Z\mathrm{r-}Z\mathrm{r}$ distance of edge-sharing $Z\mathbf{r}$ -polyhedra $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{d}=2\pi/\mathrm{q})$ , abundant in the polymerized specie. Polymerization is common for $\mathrm{Zr/Hf_{4}}$ if acid is not added to prevent hydrolysis reactions,35 and highlights the consequence of highly acidic metal centers such as $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ on solution speciation. The $\mathsf{p H}$ of aqueous solutions of redissolved $\mathrm{Zr}_{4},$ (and $\mathrm{ZrT_{d}}$ and $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ discussed later) ranges from $1.5\cdot3$ , decreasing with increased concentration of metal centers, indicating hydrolysis reactions. The 50 mmolar $(\mathrm{Zr})$ aqueous $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ solution has a $\mathsf{p H}$ of 1.39. We fit the experimental scattering curve in methanol with a $6.6\times$ $23\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ cylindrical model, consistent with the three-tetramer chain (Figure $\mathbf{3B}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). Finally, the coulombic peak between $\scriptstyle\mathbf{q=o.l-o.}5$ $\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{\scriptscriptstyle-1}$ (DMF and water) indicates ordering of clusters in solution.  Other than this coulombic peak, the scattering curves are similar to that of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ dissolved in methanol. The lack of this peak in the methanol solution is why we chose it for careful modeling, but we can assume, based on the SAXS data, that the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -connectivity and speciation is similar in all three studied solutions. The oligomeric forms of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ observed by SAXS are clearly fragmented by ESI-MS analysis (Figures. SI6, SI9-34; Table ${\bf S I}_{5}\ {\bf\&\ 6})$ . Interestingly, $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ dissolved in methanol converts to anionic methoxy-species, consistent with the strong Lewis-Brønsted acid behavior of $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ . Monomer, dimer, trimer, and tetramer species were observed. In water, $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ are predominantly $^{1+}$ tetramers. The higher degree of
fragmentation of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ in methanol than water is clearly a result of the ionization process.
SAXS of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in MeOH and DMF are consistent with the scattering data simulated from the $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ crystal structure, suggesting it dissolves intact (Figure $\mathbf{4A}$ ). The second plateau at $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{q}}={\mathsf{o}}.5^{-1.0{\mathrm{~}}{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}}^{-1}}$ in the simulated data is generally observed for non-spherical clusters with less electron density in its core. In the experimental data, this plateau is ‘smeared out’, perhaps indicating association of perchlorate ions in this small cavity. ESI-MS peaks for $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in methanol were again only observed in the negative ionization mode with a 1- charge, and were assigned predominantly to $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ clusters (Table SI7, Figures SI7, SI35-45), a result of ionization-promoted fragmentation. On the other hand, the shift of the Guinier region (to higher-q) in the scattering data for $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ dissolved in water indicates fragmentation (solution $\mathrm{pH}{=}2.36$ ). The scattering data for $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in water was satisfactorily fit using a twophase spheroidal model (radii of 7.9Å $(13\%)$ and $3.2\bar{\mathrm{A}}\left(86\%\right)$ respectively, Figure $\mathbf{4B}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). The ${\sim}8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ radius fits precisely with intact $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ while the ${\sim}3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ radius is consistent with any number of cluster fragments, i.e. trimers, tetramers and pentamers. The ESI-MS of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in water is dominated by $\mathrm{Zr}$ trimers and pentamers, detected in the positive ionization mode (Table SI8, Figure SI7, SI46-56).
SAXS analysis of the $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ reaction solution (1 molar $\mathrm{Zr}$ in the form of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ perchlorate with a 1:1 $Z\mathbf{r}$ :peroxide ratio) by inspection is remarkably similar to $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ dissolved in water, consisting mostly of smaller cluster fragments (Figure 5A). However, a $^2$ -phase fit of the freshly prepared solution (day 1), day 3, and day $^4$ (in contact with growing crystals) while evaporating approximately half the solution volume showed a distinct trend (Table 1). As the $Z\mathbf{r}$ concentration increased with water evaporation and peroxide concentration decreased with evaporation/decomposition (Zr:peroxide ${\bf\tau}=23.$ ), the smaller clusters increased in radius from ${\sim}1.5$ to $3{\cdot}5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , and the ratio of this population increased. The larger clusters increased in size from 4.5 to $8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , and the population decreased from 40 to $13\%$ . The final sizes and distribution of sizes at day 4 (supersaturated solution) matches that of redissolved crystals of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ .
Pair distribution function (PDF) analysis of $\mathbf{x}$ -ray total scattering gave more detailed information of atom-pair correlations within the clusters in the reaction solutions (Figure ${\bf5B}_{,}^{\prime}$ ). At both day 1 and $^4$ , the Cl-O correlation of perchlorate the $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ (2.2 Å) correlation of all cluster fragments, and the $Z\mathrm{r}{-}Z\mathrm{r}$ correlation peak $(3{\cdot}5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ , i.e. the edge-sharing $\mathrm{Zr}$ in $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ ) are all observed at similar intensities, when intensity-normalized to the Cl-O peak. Consistent with the SAXS data, none of the strong higher distance peaks expected for the complete $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ cluster in solution are present. We surmise that in this high $\mathrm{Zr}\left(\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\rho}}}_{1}\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\rho}}}\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\rho}}}\mathbf{\mathbf{\mathbf{\rho}}}\right)$ concentration solution, the polymerization observed prior in $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ solution (50 mmolar, no peroxide) is overcome by fragmentation via peroxide coordination and disassembly.
Although the studied reaction solutions are in contact with growing crystals, $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ never dominates the solutions. On one hand, this means $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ may assemble in the solidstate lattice via addition of small cluster fragments, rather
than the expected crystal-growth mechanism of fullyformed clusters assembling with their counterions and lattice solvent. This has been observed prior for $\mathrm{Cr-Al}$ and Cr polycations,41,42 where high acidity and/or excess nitrate prevents fully formed clusters from persisting in solution. Cluster assembly at the solid-liquid interface may become an emergent phenomenon as we continue to design synthetic strategies to isolate metastable aqueous-clusters, outside of standard $\mathsf{p H}$ control. The reason for the lack of fully formed $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in aqueous solution is not solubility (i.e.
due to large size or low charge), since $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ can be dissolved intact in less polar organic solvents. Rather, the high acidity of the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -centers likely leads to disassembly of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in water. Fully formed $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ is only ${\sim}13\%$ of all dissolved species, in equilibrium with smaller fragments. We are not able to accurately measure the acidity of the reaction solutions for either $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ or $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ (discussed below) because it is too low.
SAXS analysis of dissolved $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ perchlorate crystals (50 mmolar $Z\mathbf{r}$ , $\mathrm{\pH{=}1}.60\mathrm{\},$ ) shows that the species in solution, like $\mathbf{Zr}_{4},$ assemble into larger oligomers (Figure 6A). The scattering curve in methanol was fit with a $6.2\mathring\mathrm{A}\times21\mathring\mathrm{A}$ cylindrical model (Figure $\mathbf{SI}_{3}\mathrm{,}$ ). The mass spectral analysis in methanol and water also supports the formation of larger species in solution. Peak envelopes were assigned to a variety of negatively (MeOH) and positively (water) charged $\mathrm{Zr}_{3},\mathrm{Zr}_{4},\mathrm{Zr}_{5},$ and $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ species (Table SI9-10 Figure SI8, $\pmb{S157^{-64}}$ for methanol, $\mathbf{SI65–73}$ for water). In all of the formulae determined from the ESI-MS data, the peroxide and hydroxyl (as well as oxo) ligands are still abundant. We envision the tetrahedral clusters can link via hydrolysis and condensation of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -centers, as is proposed for $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ . Again, this is due to the acid behavior of the metal centers that converts aqua ligands to reactive OH- ligands, indicated by the low $\mathsf{p H}$ of the solution.
The $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ reaction solution initially contains 0.5 molar $\mathrm{Zr}$ as the $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ perchlorate salt, a 10:1 peroxide:Zr ratio (15 ${\bf w t\%}$ peroxide). SAXS of this reaction solution revealed an entirely different disassembly-reassembly pathway than that of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ . Initially, monodisperse spherical particles form, approximately 12 Å in diameter (Figure 6B, Table 2). This differs from the $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ solutions which exhibit elongated particles that are consistent with ‘ladder’ polymerization of the square clusters. The difference between the $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ solution and $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ reaction solution is apparent between $\scriptstyle{\mathbf{q=o.1-1.5}}{\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}~}}$ : the descent from the plateau region is shallower for $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ . Additionally, the $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ solution scattering, particularly at day 4, exhibits a distinct oscillation, generally indicative of spheroidal species.
We simulated scattering data from numerous reported group IV, lanthanide and $\mathrm{An}(\mathrm{IV})$ clusters and cluster fragments, both ligated and unligated, since these have similar cluster and oxide forms. The best match we found is with $\mathrm{Pu}_{22}.$ , consisting of 22 Pu polyhedra linked and terminated by oxo and chloride (Figure SI4).43 The $\mathrm{Pu}_{22}$ cluster is structurally very similar to a fragment of cubic $\mathrm{PuO}_{2}.$ , isostructural with $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2},$ and distinctly contains the common molecular hexamer25 building unit of the $\mathrm{Zr^{-}}$ MOF, UiO-66.26 Moreover, the $\mathrm{Pu}_{22}$ cluster, the cubic oxide, and other related, larger nuclearity $\mathrm{An(IV)}44^{-}47$ clusters contain the oxo-centered tetrahedral unit, $\mathrm{OM}_{4}$ (Figure 1) of $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ . This result was initially unexpected: we anticipated the high peroxide content in this solution would immediately disassemble the tetramers, rather than promote growth of larger clusters.
We believe this is related to peroxide decomposition, because abundant $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ bubble formation was observed in these early stages of assembly. To determine if the rapid peroxide decomposition is promoted by acid (not expected) or $Z\mathbf{r}$ , we compare peroxide loss in the $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ . reaction solution to that of a solution containing just peroxide and perchloric acid, and no dissolved Zr. After 39 days, the $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ . reaction solution has $27\%$ peroxide remaining (most of the decomposition occurs during the first few days) while the peroxide- ${\mathrm{\cdotHClO}}_{4}$ solution has $84\%$ remaining (determined by permanganate assay). While redox-active metals are known to decompose peroxide, the activity of redox inert metals such as $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ is not wellunderstood and will be a topic of future study. This finding is also important to nuclear fuel reprocessing. Zirconium is both a nuclear fuel cladding metal, and a major isotope fission product, and peroxide is formed via $\upalpha$ -hydrolysis of water in acidic fuel reprocessing solutions.
The peroxide disproportionation reaction is decribed as: $2\mathrm{O}_{2}{^{2-}}\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(\mathbf{g})+2\mathbf{O}^{2-}$ . In water, the resulting oxo (or hydroxyl) ligand will rapidly coordinate to oxophillic Zr, and hydrolysis and condensation reactions will promote assembly of polynuclear species. In the $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ reaction solution, the initial acid concentration is 1 molar when ‘prototype $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}^{\ ,}$ is observed, and it is not possible to accurately measure the very low $\mathsf{p H}$ (i.e. below 0). Additionally, peroxide decomposition does not increase the $\mathsf{p H}$ to a measurable value, because the resulting O/OH ligands immediately bind to $Z\mathbf{r}$ .
Perhaps ‘prototype $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}^{\ ,}$ could also be formed by adding base to an aqueous $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ solution, but initial studies show it is a polydisperse mixture that aggregates and precipitates. The role of the peroxide in addition to driving hydrolysis/condensation reactions is stabilizing the prenucleation clusters in solution by capping, as is observed for group $\mathrm{v}$ polyoxometalates.48 Isolation of this initial form is a subject of future study, by replacing the capping peroxide with more stable terminal ligands.
After ten days when the solution is reduced to a volume of $10\%$ the initial volume and the bubbling has subsided, the SAXS data completely changes. There is extremely high concentration of species (5 molar based on volume reduction), indicated by the large structure factor (Table 2, Figure 6B). The scattering species are very small $(-3\mathring\mathrm{A}$ diameter), indicating the initial large cluster forms were disassembled by peroxide, but likely preserving the oxocentered tetrahedron. We were not able to obtain sufficient quality PDF data on the early nucleation stages of the solutions due to the bubble formation from the peroxide, but PDF of the solution at day 10 helped interpret the SAXS data, and provided evidence for the $\mathrm{OZr}_{4}$ unit (figure $\mathsf{S I}_{5}\mathrm{\overline{{\Omega}}}$ ).
In addition to the Cl-O distance of perchlorate and the $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ bond distance at $2.2\textup{\AA}$ , we observe the strong $Z\mathbf{r}–Z\mathbf{r}$ correlation, present in $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ , and larger clusters containing the oxo-centered building unit (Figure SI5).  All pair correlations present in the simulated PDF for $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}},$ , are qualitatively present, indicating $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ , is fully formed in solution. Finally, $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ can be converted to $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ by dissolution and addition of more peroxide (10:1 peroxide:Zr), illustrating the robustness of this cluster form (isolated in $90\%$ yield), and its reaction pathway.
## CONCLUSION
Group IV $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ polycations are exploited in nanolithography for high resolution microcircuits, are the building block for MOFs and important catalysts. They have lacked the richness in structural diversity and nuclearity that has made its periodic table neighbors, polyoxometalates, an expansive class of molecular materials with tunable properties. Namely, $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ square tetramers $(\mathbf{Zr}_{4})$ and cubic hexamers have dominated the solution phase chemistry, and materials formed from the solution phase. We have shown that the compositionally simple but complexly reactive peroxide ligand can disassemble these stable building units, yielding unprecedented cluster topologies, including $\mathbf{Zr}_{25},$ the largest $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ polycation to date, yet is readily dissolved in organic solvent. Using this cluster and its smaller fragments to build cluster-based materials is one future pursuit. The reaction pathway of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ to peroxide ligated oligomers (with $^3$ to 6 nuclearity) to $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ in water is partially reversible, controlled by the self-buffering pH of dissolved Zr. On the other hand, the conversion of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ to the oxo-centered tetrahedron $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ goes through formation of larger cluster assemblies that are yet to be isolated and identified. This study provides a new mechanism and dimension to expanding $\mathrm{Zr/Hf}$ cluster chemistry via peroxide-promoted disassembly-reassembly.
In addition, the bridging motif of the peroxide ligand observed in $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ and $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ provides structure and reactivity models for $\mathrm{Zr/Hf.}$ -sulfate-peroxide lithographic processes (a topic of future study). The high solubility of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ and $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}},$ the yet-to-be isolated reaction intermediates, and analogous Hf chemistry all provide future direction and opportunity in group IV solution and solid-state chemistry.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis
$\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ : Commercial zirconium basic carbonate (ZBC, Alfa Aesar, Lot X09A021, Hf unseparated, about $1\mathrm{mol}\%\ \mathrm{Hf/Zr}$ , assayed at 41.5 wt $\%$ $(\mathrm{Zr{+}H f})\mathrm{O}_{2}.$ , $\mathbf{12.29\mathrm{g}}$ (0.0377 mol $\mathrm{Zr{+}H f)}$ ), was added to a mixture of 25 mL $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ (deionized, ${\bf\nabla}^{18\ M\Omega}$ ) $\mathbf{\Sigma}+\ 12.20\ \mathrm{g}$ of 62.1 wt $\%$ perchloric acid, warmed to $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Dissolution was complete within $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ .  The volume of the final solution was $37~\mathrm{mL}$ .  Since neither the metal nor acid components are affected by subsequent evaporation steps, we regard this parent solution as being 1.02 M $\mathrm{Zr{+}H f}$ and with perchlorate $\mathrm{|Zr{+}H f}=2.0$ . The $\mathsf{p H}$ of this reaction solution (and that of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ and $\mathrm{ZrT_{d}}$ , described below) is too low to measure accurately (i.e. below 0). An aliquot of this was allowed to evaporate in air until dendritic crystals of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ were formed. These were taken for $\mathbf{\deltaX}^{}$ -ray crystallographic determination. The Zr and Hf composition is 99.3 and $0.7\%$ (Table SI1), a slight Hf decrease from the starting material $({-2\%}\mathrm{Hf})$ .
$\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ : To 0.50 mL of the parent solution was added $52\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 30 wt $\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ giving a starting mole ratio peroxide $/\mathrm{Zr}{+}\mathrm{Hf}=1.0$ .  The solution was contained in a small polyethylene cup and was allowed to evaporate into the ambient atmosphere.  After four days, with about $54\%$ loss of initial weight (water and peroxide), clear crystals in the form of prisms and lozenges, appeared, amounting to about $10\%$ of the initial metal values. These were taken for X-ray crystallographic determination.
$\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ : To 1.00 mL of the parent solution was added 1.00 mL of 30 wt $\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , giving a starting mole ratio peroxide $/\mathrm{Zr}+\mathrm{Hf}=9.6$ .  This was allowed to evaporate into ambient air for $_\mathrm{~4~d~}$ after which it was placed into a dessicator over concentrated ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ .  After 14 d and loss of $80\%$ of the initial mass, no solids had formed, so dessication over $\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ was employed.  This produced a mostly solid mass which re-hydrated rapidly in air with disappearance of solids.  Re-placement over $\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ resulted in a mixture of cylindrical crystals, ${\sim}0.2\ \mathrm{mm}$ by $\mathbf{1}{-}5\mathbf{mm}$ , in contact with liquor.  From this assemblage, the diffraction sample was taken.  The final weight of the assemblage was 0.44 g compared to an estimated (from eventual crystallographic formula weight) weight of $\mathbf{0.35~g}$ of $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ solids plus $\mathbf{0.05~g}$ “excess” perchloric $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{\iotacid}}=\mathbf{o.40}\mathbf{\sigmag}$ .  In a parallel experiment, the evaporating mixture was analyzed for peroxide and found to have peroxide $/\mathrm{Zr}=0.52$ (0.50 found by x-ray in final solid), indicating that the large initial excess of peroxide had evaporated or decomposed, leaving the expected final stoichiometry.
We note that despite the use of the same starting materials, the synthesis of $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ (with a much larger excess of peroxide) was prolonged and high-yielding, with dryout-re-hydrate steps; in contrast to $\mathbf{Zr}_{25},$ which is comparatively rapid and low-yield.  The crystal morphologies of the two are noticeably different: rhombohedra for $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{25},$ and elongated cylinders for the former.  Attempts to increase the yield of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ by forcing conditions of strong dessication or even mild warming $(45^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , resulted in disruption of the crystals. This causes us to posit that the former phase is the more-stable one.
Characterization Techniques
Single crystal $X$ -ray diffraction: Data of $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ were collected on a Bruker DUO-APEX II CCD area detector at 171 K using a $\mathtt{C u}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54178\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ ). Data reduction was accomplished using SAINT V8.34a.49 The substantial redundancy in data allowed a semiempirical absorption correction (SADABS $V_{2.10})^{50}$ to be applied, based on multiple measurements of equivalent reflections. In the case of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25},$ data were collected at 100.0(1) K on a Rigaku Oxford Diffraction Synergy-S equipped with a PhotonJet-S Mo source $\left(\lambda=0.71073\bar{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ ) and HyPix-6000HE photon counting detector. All the images were collected and processed using CrysAlisPro Version 1.171.40.20a (Rigaku Oxford Diffraction, 2018). After integration, both numerical (Gaussian) absorption and empirical absorption (spherical harmonic, image scaling and detector scaling) corrections were applied.
All the structures were solved by the intrinsic phasing method from the SHELXT program, developed by successive difference Fourier syntheses, and refined by fullmatrix least squares on all $\mathrm{F}_{2}$ data using SHELX51 via $_{\mathrm{OLEX}_{2}5^{2}}$ interface. The crystal data of the three compounds are given in Table SI2. ICSD file numbers: 1891906 $(\mathbf{Zr}_{4})$ -1891907 $(\mathbf{Zr}_{25})$ -1898479 $(\mathbf{ZrT_{d}})$ .
Small Angle $X$ -ray Scattering (SAXS): X-ray scattering data were collected on an Anton Paar SAXSess instrument using $\mathrm{Cu-K}\upalpha$ radiation $(\mathbf{1.54\textup{\AA}})$ and line collimation. The instrument was equipped with a 2-D image plate for data collection in the $\mathbf{q}~=~\mathbf{o.o18-2.5}~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}$ range. The lower q resolution is limited by the beam attenuator. Approximately 50 mmolar $\left(\mathrm{Zr}\right)$ solutions were measured in $\mathbf{1.5\mm}$ glass capillaries (Hampton Research). Scattering data of neat solvent was collected for background subtraction. Scattering was measured for 30 min for every experiment. We used SAXSQUANT software for data collection and treatment (normalization, primary beam removal, background subtraction, desmearing, and smoothing to remove the extra noise created by the desmearing routine). All analyses and curve-fitting to determine $\mathrm{Rg}$ , size, shape and size distribution were carried out utilizing IRENA macros with IgorPro 6.3 (Wavemetrics) software.53 To simulate scattering data from the crystal structure, we used SolX software.54
Powder $X\mathrm{.}$ -ray diffraction: Powder XRD of compounds $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ and $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ were collected to ensure the single crystal is representative of the bulk. The data were collected on a Miniflex 600 in the range from $^3$ to $30^{\circ}\ \left(2\theta\right)$ with a scan speed of $5^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ and step  size of $\mathbf{0.02}^{\circ}$ . $\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ was mixed with paratone oil to limit its the deliquescent behavior (figure $\mathrm{SI74},$ ). For similar reason, crystals of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25}$ were not ground, leading to a preferential orientation (figure $\mathsf{S I75}$ ).
Electrospray Ionization Mass Spectrometry (ESI-MS): ESI-MS was carried out using an Agilent 6230 ESI-MS system comprised of a Time-of-Flight (TOF) mass spectrometer coupled to an electrospray ionizer. Samples of $\mathbf{Zr}_{25},\mathbf{ZrT_{d}}$ and $\mathbf{Zr}_{4}$ $[\mathrm{Zr}]{=}1.0\ \mathrm{mM})$ were dissolved in neat water and neat methanol and infused into the ESI-MS system at a flow rate of 0.4 mL min-1 using a syringe pump. The solutions were nebulized with the aid of heated $\Nu_{2}$ (325 ${}^{\circ}C)$ flowing at $^{8\mathrm{L}}$ min-1 and a pressure of 35 psig $(241\mathrm{kPa})$ . The voltages of the capillary, skimmer, and RT octopole were set at 3500, 65, and $_{750\mathrm{~V~}}$ respectively, while the voltage of the fragmenter was set at 100V. The data were collected in both the positive and negative ionization modes.
Pair Distribution Function (PDF) analysis of X-ray total scattering: Solution measurements were performed with Rigaku Smartlab X-ray diffractometer with Mo- $\mathtt{K a}$ irradiation $(\lambda{=}0.7{\bf10}73\mathrm{~}\mathring\mathrm{A};$ ) in the $\phantom{-}2\theta$ range of $3.0{-}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}8.5^{\circ}$ with transmission geometry. Solution samples were loaded in Kapton capillary with $1.46~\mathrm{~mm}$ inner diameter for irradiation. The theoretical $q$ -range is up to a $q_{m a x}$ of 15.5 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ . A 0.2 degree/minute data collection time and $\mathrm{D}/\mathrm{teX}$ Ultra-HE high-speed one-dimensional X-ray detector were utilized to ensure high quality scattering data. PDFgetX355 was to process scattering data into the corresponding PDF. The corresponding simulations were created in PDFgui.56
Scanning  Electron  Microscopy  (SEM)  and  Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDS): Electron micrographs and atomic ratios $(\%)$ of the crystalline materials were obtained from a Quanta 600F instrument  (FEI) combining  a  scanning  electron  microscope  and  an energy-dispersive  X-ray spectrometer.
